    "lawCode": "Amd §906, N-PC L; amd §12, Rel Corp L",
    "lawSection": "Not-for-Profit Corporation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5720\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the not-for-profit corporation law and\nthe religious corporations law, in relation to the correction of tax\nerrors made by the nonprofit revitalization act\n\n \nSUMMARY OF MAJOR PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one would correct an obvious error in Not-for-Profit Corporation\nLaw section 906(a) (1)(D) to change the cross reference from paragraph\n(c) of section 607 to paragraph (b). There is no paragraph (c).\n\nSection two would amend Religious Corporations Law section 12.1 to\ndelete a reference to section 511-a of the Not-for-Profit Corporation l\nlaw. Section 12.1 was amended to add the phrase proposed to be deleted\nby Section 17 Chapter 555 of the 2015 Laws.\n\nSection three provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPrior to its amendment, Section 12.1 was unequivocal in requiring court\napproval of any religious corporation's proposed sale, lease or mortgage\nof \"any of its real property\" (subject to certain minor exceptions),\npursuant to Not-for-Profit corporation Law Section 511 which in turn\nprovides for the Attorney General approval in the Court's discretion.\n\nMoreover, under Religious Corporations Law Section 2-b (d-1), which\n\"borrows\" certain provisions of the Not-for-Profit Corporation Law, the\nchurches specified therein are exempt even from the Section 511 Court\napproval requirement.\n\nReligious Corporation Law section 2-b.1 establishes in general the\nprimacy of the Religious Corporation Law over the Not-for-Profit Corpo-\nration Law.\n\nThe purpose of Not-for-Profit Corporation Law Section 511-a was to\nprovide for not-for-profit corporations the alternative of Attorney\nGeneral approval for Court approval.\n\nAs amended by Section 17 of Chapter 555 of the 2015 Laws, Sections 12.1\nand 2-b(d-1) are in conflict.\n\nAs the annotators of 2018 McKinney's Not-for-Profit Corporation Law\npocket part concluded:\n\nGiven the primacy afforded to the RCL, the mandatory breadth of section\n12, the public policy favoring the protection of religious corporation\nproperty and the exemption of hierarchical (as contrasted with congrega-\ntional) churches from the Attorney General notice requirement, the\nbetter view would seem to be that NPCL section 511-a should be inappli-\ncable to the hierarchical religious corporations (and perhaps even to\ncongregational ones)....The RCL should be amended to delete the amend-\nment made by Chapter 555.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill; May of 2019.\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediate.\n",
